Harmonization across studies is experimental: \code{\link[=qes_spec]{qes_spec()}} shows the
reviewed spec, \code{\link[=qes_harmonize]{qes_harmonize()}} applies it and \code{\link[=qes_design]{qes_design()}} turns the
result into a survey design; the reference generated from the spec is
\code{vignette("harmonization-reference", package = "qesR")}. \code{\link[=qes_decon]{qes_decon()}}
is the relaxed version: one column per concept for every study, in coarse
common categories and with no grade.

Older function names (\code{get_codebook()}, \code{get_question()},
\code{get_preview()}, \code{get_qescodes()}, ...) keep working and will not be
removed; \link{qesR-deprecated} gives the replacement of each one.

Guides: \code{vignette("get-started", package = "qesR")} (from a study code to a
weighted estimate), \code{vignette("citations", package = "qesR")},
\code{vignette("migrating-0.7", package = "qesR")} (upgrading older code) and
\code{vignette("harmonization-reference", package = "qesR")} (the reference
generated from the harmonization spec). The website,
\url{https://thomasgareau.github.io/qesR/}, also has the study catalog and
analysis examples built from the full data files, in English and French.
}

\section{Returning data}{

Every function returns its result visibly and, by default, writes nothing
into your workspace: write \code{qes2018 <- get_qes("qes2018")}. Functions with
an \code{assign_global} argument assign only when it is \code{TRUE}, and then into the
environment they were called from (the global environment only when called
at top level).
}

